To model an 8-bit shift register in Verilog, you can use a module with a register array and shift operations. Here's an example implementation:

```verilogmodule shift_reg_8bit(input clk, input rst, output [7:0] data_out, output reg [7:0] data_in)
  reg [7:0] shift_reg [7:0];

  always @(posedge clk or posedge rst)
  begin
    if (rst)
      data_in <= 0;
    else if (clk)
    begin
      data_in <= shift_reg[7];
      for (int i = 7 downto 0)
        shift_reg[i] <= shift_reg[i+1];
      data_out <= shift_reg[0];
    end
  end
endmodule
```

This module has a register array `shift_reg` to store the current state of the register, and input and output signals `clk` (clock), `rst` (reset), `data_out` (output data), and `data_in` (input data). The always block is sensitive to clock or reset events. When reset is high, the register is reset to zero. When the clock is high, the input data is stored in the most significant register (`shift_reg[7]`), and then the remaining registers are shifted one position to the left, with the new value stored in the previous register. The output data is the value in the first register, `shift_reg[0]`.

